--TEST--
`Core\Fatal::onUncaughtThrow`'s handler is handed the very `Throwable` that went uncaught — the same object, not a report built from it — and nothing after the throw runs — `rule:errors/on-uncaught-throw`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:errors/on-uncaught-throw`'s load-bearing sentence is that tier 2 gets "the **real
// `Throwable` object**, not copied data": this is the request's own root, not
// an `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing` isolate boundary that has to copy across. A ladder that rebuilt
// the exception — or that handed over a keyed array the way § 1's `LimitReport`
// is one — answers every other question here identically and fails on the one
// line below that asks about identity.
//
// `rule:expressions/object-identity-equality` makes `==` on two objects identity, so this is the whole test:
// the exception is built here, captured by the handler, and thrown from two
// frames down.
var $error = new LogicError("the store said no");

Core\Fatal::onUncaughtThrow(fn (Throwable $seen): void => {
    echo "same object: ", $seen == $error ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
    // And it is a live object rather than a husk: the ordinary members read
    // what the program threw, including the frames unwinding recorded on the
    // way up — which is what tells a handler *where* rather than only *what*.
    echo "message: ", $seen->message, "\n";
    echo "frames: ", Core\Arr::count($seen->backtrace), "\n";
});

class Boom {
    public static function inner(Throwable $error): void {
        throw $error;
    }

    public static function outer(Throwable $error): void {
        Boom::inner($error);
    }
}

echo "start\n";
Boom::outer($error);
// § 2 fires at the *root*, which is reached only because every frame between
// here and the throw declined to catch it. Control does not come back.
echo "never\n";
--EXPECT--
start
same object: yes
message: the store said no
frames: 3
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
{"level":"error","msg":"the store said no","fields":{"class":"LogicError"},"nodes":%s}
